<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aude (river)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aude is a river in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, about 224 km long. It rises in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> and flows north through Quillan and Limoux to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carcassonne">Carcassonne</a>, then east to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a>. In antiquity its valley and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a> formed a trade route across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> between the Mediterranean and the Atlantic. It gave its name to the Aude <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aude-river.jpg" alt="Aude (river)" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Pol GRANDMONT · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carcassonne_JPG01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Aude rises at the Lac d'Aude, at 2,185 metres in the Massif of Carlit, in the commune of Les Angles in Pyrénées-Orientales. It flows parallel to the Tet, and the Col de la Quillane, at 1,713 metres, marks the edge of its watershed. In its upper course it runs through Capcir, feeding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> such as Matemale and Puyvalador, and plunges into gorges, of which Saint-Georges is the most scenic. From Axat, where it is joined by the Aiguette and the Rébenty, it crosses limestone in the Pierre-Lys Gorges and passes Quillan, Espéraza, Couiza and Alet-les-Bains to reach Limoux.</p><p>Below Limoux the river turns east. This elbow results from stream capture: the Aude once flowed in the valley of the Hers. From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carcassonne">Carcassonne</a> it follows the great tectonic furrow between the Corbières and the Montagne Noire, receiving the Orbieu, the Argent-Double and the Cesse, and runs beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canal-du-midi">Canal du Midi</a> among vineyards to the plain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a>. It reaches the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> at the Grau de Vendres, on the border of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> of Aude and Hérault. Its catchment covers 6,074 square kilometres across six departments.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> called the river Atax, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> and other writers called it Narbôn. Medieval texts used forms such as Adice, Fluvium Atacis and Juxta Aditum fluvium, and a cartulary of 1342 calls it the Auda or Ribera d'Aude. The present name probably evolved from Atax, as given by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, a word borrowed from the Gallic term atacos, meaning spirited or very fast. The river gave its name to the Aude department.</p><p>Its flow is mostly governed by rain, and it can rise suddenly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> autumn. On 12 and 13 November 1999 up to 620 millimetres of rain fell in two days at Lézignan-Corbières, more than the average annual total, while a storm in the Gulf of Lion raised the sea by 80 centimetres and held back the swollen river. The floods killed 35 people and ruined about 5,000 hectares of vineyards. In 2018 remnants of Hurricane Leslie fed a stalled cold front and caused flash floods; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carcassonne">Carcassonne</a> received 160 to 180 millimetres of rain within five hours, the Aude rose 7 metres, its highest level since 1891, and at least 14 people died, mainly at Villegailhenc.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aude (river)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Burdigala was a trading port linking the Atlantic with the Mediterranean by the Garonne and <b>Aude</b> route, handling tin, metals and Italian wine.</blockquote><a href="/state/bituriges-vivisci">Bituriges Vivisci</a></li></ul></section>
